WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.477
Presenting results to people who don’t read confidence intervals.

2
00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:11.075
You will present eval results to people who make decisions without reading code: a VP, a client, a compliance lead.

3
00:00:11.355 --> 00:00:13.723
You already know this audience from PM work.

4
00:00:14.003 --> 00:00:23.816
The eval adds three translation problems: metric names mean nothing to them, uncertainty is easy to drop, and averages hide the failures they care about.

5
00:00:24.096 --> 00:00:25.440
Lead with the decision.

6
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:31.309
“I recommend shipping the reranker” or “not ready: it invents policy details too often.

7
00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:32.784
” Then the evidence.

8
00:00:33.064 --> 00:00:36.968
Translate metrics into user outcomes, with natural frequencies.

9
00:00:37.248 --> 00:00:38.827
Not “faithfulness 0.

10
00:00:39.107 --> 00:00:48.088
88,” but “about 88 answers in 100 stick to what the handbook says; in the rest, the assistant adds something the handbook doesn’t say.

11
00:00:48.368 --> 00:00:50.288
” Not “recall@5 of 0.

12
00:00:50.568 --> 00:00:58.995
81,” but “for about 1 question in 5, the system doesn’t find the right page, and then it either says it doesn’t know or guesses.

13
00:00:59.275 --> 00:00:59.572
”.

14
00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:02.348
Say how sure you are, in words, with the range.

15
00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:08.495
“Somewhere between 82 and 93 in 100, based on 120 test questions.

16
00:01:08.775 --> 00:01:14.130
” If the interval includes “no change,” say “we can’t tell yet” rather than reporting a gain.

17
00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:15.690
Show two examples.

18
00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.895
One answer that is now right and was wrong, one failure that remains.

19
00:01:20.175 --> 00:01:25.466
Examples are what people remember, and the remaining failure sets expectations honestly.

20
00:01:25.746 --> 00:01:27.154
Cover cost and risk.

21
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:38.420
Cost per answer and per month, latency, and what the eval does not cover (questions about topics not in the set, adversarial users, the abstention rate on real traffic).

22
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.626
End with what would change your recommendation, and when you will report again.

23
00:01:42.906 --> 00:01:49.370
This turns a one-time number into an operating rhythm, which is what leaders actually want from an eval program.

24
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:57.052
Put metric definitions, intervals, judge calibration, and the experiment log in an appendix for the technical reviewers.

25
00:01:57.332 --> 00:02:04.138
The lab’s report structure (bottom line first, details after, caveats last) is a template for exactly this.
